{"version":1,"kind":"summary","errors":154,"warnings":0,"explanations":{"E0032":"# E0032: value of the wrong type\n\nA value must have the type its position expects: the declared type of a `let`, the parameter of a function, the return type of a function, the operand of an operator.\n\n## Why\n\nSisu does not convert between number types by itself. A `u8`, a `char`, an `i64` and an `f64` are different types even when the number would fit, so a program says where one becomes another with `u8(...)`, `i64(...)`, `f64(...)` or `char(...)`. A `u8` and a `char` convert through `i64`: `char(i64(b))`, and `f64(i64(b))`. A conversion takes one type: `i64(...)` takes a `u8`, a `char` or an `f64`, and `u8(...)`, `char(...)` and `f64(...)` take an `i64`. An `i64` beside an `f64` in arithmetic or a comparison, as in `n * x`, is the one reported, with the unsafe fix to convert it: `f64(n) * x`. Where the context wants an `i64`, as `n += x` does, the `f64` is the one reported, and no fix converts it. A value that does not fit makes `u8(...)` or `char(...)` fail, and an `f64` that is not a whole number in `i64`'s range makes `i64(...)` fail, so each gives an optional, a `u8?`, a `char?` or an `i64?`: give a default with `??`, or unwrap it with `if let`. The fix that converts is offered only where that optional is expected.\n\nA value converts to an optional through one `?` only: `let x: i64? = 5` is valid, but `let x: i64?? = 5` is not. Wrap the value in `Some(...)` for each further layer: `let x: i64?? = Some(5)`.\n\nA value converts to an interface value, `any Shape`, when its type conforms to `Shape` and it is not an optional. An `i64?` is no `any Display`, and a `Circle?` is no `any Shape` and no `(any Shape)?`. Unwrap the optional with `if let` or `??`, then convert the value. A `Circle` converts to `(any Shape)?` and is wrapped.\n\nA `panic` or `todo` message is a `string`. An `i64`, `u8`, `f64`, `bool` or `char` written on one line becomes one with interpolation: `panic(n)` is `panic(\"{n}\")`, and `todo(n)` is `todo(\"{n}\")`.\n\n## Broken\n\n```sisu\nfn main() {\n    let n: i64 = 5\n    let b: u8 = n\n    print(b)\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed\n\n```sisu\nfn main() {\n    let n: i64 = 5\n    let b: u8 = u8(n) ?? 0\n    print(b)\n}\n```\n","E0033":"# E0033: name that is not in scope\n\nA name in an expression must be a binding in scope: a `let`, a parameter, or a binding made by `for`, `if let` or `while let`. A binding is in scope from its declaration to the end of its block.\n\nThe name of a type is no binding: `f64` or a class's name read as a value is [E0105](E0105.md). So is the name of a function read as a value.\n\nWhen a binding in scope has a similar name, the error offers it as an unsafe fix. The unsafe fix may be wrong: the name could be a binding that was never declared.\n\n## Why\n\nSisu does not resolve a misspelled name on its own. A misspelled name, or one used after its block has ended, would otherwise read some other value or none.\n\n## Broken\n\n```sisu\nfn main() {\n    let total = 3\n    print(totl)\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed\n\n```sisu\nfn main() {\n    let total = 3\n    print(total)\n}\n```\n","E0050":"# E0050: a type that cannot be inferred\n\n`None` takes the optional type its position expects. Where nothing says which type, Sisu cannot choose one.\n\n## Why\n\nEvery optional is an optional of some type: `None` of `i64?` and `None` of `Tree?` are different values. A `let` with no type has nothing to take it from.\n\nThe help of a `let` with `None` or `[]` writes the type from the first later use of the local that gives one. A use can be a call argument, a `return`, an assignment or a `push`. When no use gives a type, the help writes `...` in its place, as in `let x: ...? = None`.\n\nA call of a generic function, class or variant has the same problem when nothing fixes one of its type parameters. `first([])` gives `T` no type: the `[]` has none of its own, and a later use of the result does not count. Annotate the result, `let x: i64? = first([])`, or write the type arguments, `first<i64>([])`. A parameter that the result names only through an associated type, as `S` in `-> [S.Item]`, is not fixed by an annotation of the result: write the type arguments, `collect<Countdown>()`.\n\nAn alias whose body does not use a type parameter has the same problem. In `type F<T> = f64`, no value gives `T` a type, so the compiler cannot infer `T` at `F.pi`. Write the type argument, `F<i64>.pi`, or remove `T` from the alias, `type F = f64`. Sisu gives no warning for a type parameter that nothing uses.\n\n`Some(None)` has the same problem: `Some` holds a value of any type, and `None` has no type of its own. Annotate the optional, `let x: i64?? = Some(None)`.\n\n`Ok(1)` has the same problem: `Ok` holds the value, and nothing says the type of the error. Annotate the result, `let r: Result<i64, string> = Ok(1)`. `Err(\"bad\")` leaves the type of the value open in the same way.\n\n`x ?? None ?? 0` has the same problem, because `??` groups to the right. It is `x ?? (None ?? 0)`, and nothing gives that `None` a type. To remove two layers from an `i64??`, write `(x ?? None) ?? 0`.\n\n## Broken\n\n```sisu\nfn main() {\n    let x = None\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed\n\n```sisu\nfn main() {\n    let x: i64? = None\n    print(x ?? 0)\n}\n```\n"}}
